I’ll inspect the measurement metadata, pattern, and the provided diffraction-analysis guidance first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine a common multiphase GSAS-II model. I’ll keep residual tests inside that shared project and validate the final files with a fresh profile replay.

The metadata points to the Mn–W–O system after an 800 °C WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ reaction. The supplied lab model allows target Mn tungstates, residual oxides, and off-stoichiometric intermediates, with unknown specimen displacement, profile broadening, texture, and background; I’ll account for those rather than treating unmatched peaks as phases immediately.

The framework’s research-controller self-check reports that the workshop/evolution infrastructure is intentionally undeployed. That check governs launching the automated research process the prompt explicitly excludes; it does not block this direct case analysis. The local crystallography stack, frozen COD resources, DICVOL, and GSAS-II remain the relevant tools, so I’m continuing with those.

A first peak-by-peak check identifies monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄ unambiguously: its reference lines require a common positive angular shift of about 0.33°, and then match the dominant 15.87, 18.86, 24.05/24.61, 30.36/30.81, 31.67, 36.58, 37.85, 41.51, and higher-angle groups. Coherent extra peaks remain (notably near 20.32, 20.88, 23.50, 29.14, and 34–35.6°), so this is not yet a defensible single-phase result.

The dominant residual is ordinary monoclinic WO₃, not an exotic phase: its characteristic 23.17/23.63/24.39° triplet maps directly to the residual 23.50/23.95/24.74° group under the same specimen shift, with matching lines near 26.94, 29.14, 33.66–34.52, and 41.8–42.2°. I’m refining MnWO₄ + WO₃ together now; only the residual after that common fit will drive any prototype-transfer search.

The two-phase fit confirms WO₃ at a substantial scale and drops Rwp from 25.7% to 18.3%, but it also exposes a third coherent pattern rather than noise. Its strongest unmatched lines are 20.32, 20.88, 29.14, 35.62, 47.02, and 54.89°, with a consistent high-angle continuation. I’m now testing all ordinary Mn oxides in the unchanged MnWO₄+WO₃ state and indexing that residual before considering analogue substitution.

The controlled ordinary-phase tests are separating real and spurious candidates. Rocksalt MnO gives only a small improvement (~2.3 wt%), while tetragonal hausmannite Mn₃O₄ and one Pbcm Mn₃O₄ model collapse below 0.4 wt% in the same fixed state. A hexagonal MnO analogue improves Rwp by about one point and remains at ~11 wt%, so it warrants a full common refinement—but it still cannot explain the 20.3/20.9° doublet. One alternate Pbcm trial became numerically invalid and is being retained as unknown, not counted as a rejection.

Residual indexing and analogue screening now point to a chemically coherent off-stoichiometric phase: Mn₃WO₆. Both Mn₃TeO₆ (R‑3) and Zn₃TeO₆ (C2/c) frameworks, after Te→W and cell adjustment, reproduce the 20.3/20.9°, 29.14°, 32.26°, 35.62°, 46–47°, and 55–57° sequences. This also explains why WO₃ coexists: for a 1:1 Mn:W charge, forming Mn₃WO₆ requires two moles of excess WO₃ per mole of Mn₃WO₆. I’ll test both symmetries, plus the strongest Mn₂WO₆ alternatives, as replacements and additions in the same project.

The common-state analogue trials are running now. I’m evaluating each candidate twice: once replacing the provisional hexagonal MnO surrogate, and once adding it while retaining that phase. All background, instrument, specimen, and established-phase parameters are frozen during these tests; only the trial phase scale, cell, and physically bounded width are allowed to move.

The decisive result is in: replacing the MnO surrogate with the R‑3 Mn₃WO₆ model drops Rwp from 17.18% to 9.66% on cell refinement and 7.56% with a physical phase width (0.159 µm, ~1070 ppm strain), at ~13.8 wt%. The C2/c alternative only reaches 16.15% and demands an implausibly narrow/boundary-like profile. I’m completing the remaining symmetry controls and addition tests before selecting R‑3.

The remaining replacement controls are all substantially worse than R‑3: P2₁/c Mn₃WO₆ stays near 18%, P2₁2₁2₁ Mn₂WO₆ near 17.6%, and P‑31m Mn₂WO₆ near 16.6% while pushing profile terms to limits. This makes the R‑3 framework both the profile and physical winner; the run is now checking whether retaining the provisional MnO adds any independent intensity.

The R‑3 addition test is worse than its replacement test (9.42% versus 7.56% Rwp), so the provisional hexagonal MnO is not an independent phase; it was borrowing intensity from Mn₃WO₆. I’ll exclude it from the final inventory and resume ordinary refinement from the saved R‑3 replacement project after the last negative controls finish.

The phase inventory is now closed at three crystalline phases: MnWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and R‑3 Mn₃WO₆. I’m resuming a conventional refinement from that saved project, constraining the sample shift and profile terms to the laboratory ranges, then refining cell/scale/background, common instrument profile, phase widths, symmetry-allowed coordinates, and isotropic displacement factors in stages.

Ordinary refinement has reduced Rwp to 4.31%, and symmetry-allowed MnWO₄ coordinate recovery lowers it further to 3.52%. The common instrument fit wants U≈44.7 (slightly beyond the stated 0–40 laboratory draw), so I’ll clamp/replay that term at the allowed edge before final selection; all analytic widths remain positive.

The refined fractions independently satisfy the precursor mass balance: WO₃/Mn₃WO₆ is 1.046 by weight, essentially the 1.043 expected for the required 2:1 molar pair. I also caught an unstable WO₃ oxygen-coordinate trial that had persisted despite being marked rejected; I’m restoring those oxygen sites to the last stable checkpoint, clamping U to 40, and re-refining widths before any final export.

Physical screening passes MnWO₄ and R‑3 Mn₃WO₆. The initial monoclinic WO₃ reference has a 1.58 Å O–O contact and therefore only “needs adjudication”; an equivalent monoclinic WO₃ COD setting (2311041) passes the same screen and already ranked slightly better in the residual search. I’m testing that physically valid setting as a direct replacement in the otherwise frozen three-phase model.

The physically valid monoclinic WO₃ replacement is successful: with all shared settings frozen, COD 2311041 lowers Rwp from 3.03% to 2.17% and stays within physical size/strain ranges. The triclinic equivalent reaches 2.26%, so the monoclinic P2₁/c model is currently preferred.

The physical WO₃ setting plus ordinary full-model refinement is now at Rwp 2.06%; a W-only coordinate adjustment lowers it to 2.02% with ~0.07–0.08 Å shifts, while unconstrained WO₃ displacement factors became nonphysical and were rejected. I’ve restored the common Uiso=0.0127 and am testing only bounded specimen/texture/profile nuisances from this 2.02% state.

Texture tests so far refine to March–Dollase ratios essentially equal to 1.00 and improve Rwp by at most ~0.015 percentage points, indicating no meaningful preferred orientation in MnWO₄ or Mn₃WO₆. I’m finishing the WO₃ axes and will keep texture off unless a bounded, nontrivial ratio is supported.

The bounded nuisance tests support no texture or surface roughness. Two modest uniaxial strain terms are statistically supported and physically plausible—Mn₃WO₆ along [010] (axial/equatorial ≈1.94) and WO₃ along [010] (≈1.58)—while MnWO₄ remains isotropic. I’m combining only those terms with the refined polarization and reconverging the full three-phase model.

The combined bounded model converged at Rwp 2.005% with 68.996 wt% MnWO₄, 15.296 wt% R‑3 Mn₃WO₆, and 15.709 wt% monoclinic WO₃. Texture and roughness remain off; all retained instrument/profile terms are within the stated ranges. I’m exporting directly from this project, physically screening the exact CIFs, and then replaying the saved project over the full original grid.

The three-phase model is now refined. I’m verifying the exported CIFs against the final project, then I’ll perform the required fresh full-grid replay and generate the submitted profile, statistics, and report from that replay.

All three submitted CIFs pass the geometry/occupancy screen with no flagged short contacts. I’m now replaying the saved atomic models over all 8,301 measured points; this discards any cached calculated profile and is the final selection check.

The fresh replay succeeded with no profile-validity flags. It reproduces the refined fractions—MnWO₄ 68.996 wt%, Mn₃WO₆ 15.296 wt%, and WO₃ 15.709 wt%—and gives \(R_{wp}=2.0053\%\) over the complete measured grid.

The submission now passes the local evaluator checks: all three phase records parse, fractions sum exactly to 1.0, each CIF matches its corresponding phase exported from `final.gpx`, and `fit.csv` covers the original grid with the replayed \(R_{wp}\). I’m doing one final artifact audit now.

Completed the phase analysis and refinement.

- MnWO₄, P 2/c: 68.996 wt%
- Mn₃WO₆, R -3: 15.296 wt%
- WO₃, P 2₁/c: 15.709 wt%

Fresh full-grid GSAS-II replay: Rwp 2.0053%, Rp 1.5348%, goodness-of-fit 1.1322. All submitted CIFs passed physical screening. The analogue-derived Mn₃WO₆ coordinates carry the greatest structural uncertainty.

